I use the \citet{callaway2021difference} estimator, which computes group--time average treatment effects $ATT(g,t)$ for each adoption cohort $g$ and calendar period $t$:
\begin{equation}
    ATT(g,t) = \E[Y_t(g) - Y_t(\infty) | G = g]
\end{equation}
where $Y_t(g)$ is the potential outcome under treatment at time $g$ and $Y_t(\infty)$ is the never-treated potential outcome.

The \textit{simple aggregate} ATT averages across all post-treatment group--time cells:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\theta}^{simple} = \sum_{g} \sum_{t \geq g} w_{g,t} \cdot \widehat{ATT}(g,t)
\end{equation}
The \textit{dynamic aggregate} produces event-study estimates by averaging across cohorts for each relative time period $e = t - g$:
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\theta}(e) = \sum_{g} w_g \cdot \widehat{ATT}(g, g + e)
\end{equation}

This approach avoids the negative weighting and heterogeneity bias problems of standard two-way fixed effects (TWFE) estimators in staggered adoption settings \citep{goodman2021difference, de2020two, borusyak2024revisiting}.

